{"requirements":[{"id":"local-caching-resolver","requirement":"Applications on node1 use a functioning local caching DNS resolver.","status":"satisfied","summary":"systemd-resolved is enabled and active, /etc/resolv.conf directs applications to the loopback stub at 127.0.0.53, UDP and TCP listeners are present, and two controlled application lookups produce an observed cache hit. The explicit cache configuration and the same healthy behavior are directly observed after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bb58fa4dba1c44be","cmd-74609ff915fd418d","cmd-dc0235cb90fb40d0","cmd-a23f5212864a4538","cmd-e2b99b514647443b"]},{"id":"external-name-resolution","requirement":"Normal external name resolution remains available.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A pre-change external lookup succeeded, and external archlinux.org lookups succeeded after configuration and again after reboot. Resolver status continued to show usable global and DHCP-provided upstream DNS servers.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d4fc53b2f80d4cc3","cmd-cf8993c1da45448e","cmd-74609ff915fd418d","cmd-e2b99b514647443b"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The preliminary cache flush/statistics reset and lookups were a bounded capability and preservation probe (cmd-cf8993c1da45448e); the drop-in write implemented the requested configuration (cmd-40fe47c59bc94233); enabling/restarting applied it (cmd-bb58fa4dba1c44be); later cache resets and lookups collected bounded pre- and post-restart evidence (cmd-74609ff915fd418d, cmd-e2b99b514647443b); and the scheduled reboot fulfilled the mandatory restart protocol (cmd-dc0235cb90fb40d0). The failed verification subcommand only encountered documentation lookup errors, with no evidence of an additional mutation. Lifecycle observations show no failed services, package damage, unwanted socket exposure, or abandoned temporary files; socket and temporary-file differences are attributable to changed process IDs and boot-scoped directories.","evidence_ids":["cmd-cf8993c1da45448e","cmd-40fe47c59bc94233","cmd-bb58fa4dba1c44be","cmd-74609ff915fd418d","cmd-dc0235cb90fb40d0","cmd-e2b99b514647443b","global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied. The mandatory node-restart evidence is complete: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID were captured, the reboot was acknowledged and followed by connection loss, the post-restart boot ID changed, and system, resolver configuration, listener, caching, and external resolution recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently confirm the transition from fe6e58b2-7e4d-4e8a-b1d7-3a15564059bb to 611cec02-4b59-4696-b0b8-172d79a6e549.","limitations":["The lifecycle snapshots do not inventory resolved configuration-file contents, and the earlier cat-config output is truncated, so they cannot independently establish whether 20-local-cache.conf existed before it was written. Its intended final contents and post-reboot behavior are nevertheless directly observed.","DNS behavior was validated through representative getent lookups rather than an exhaustive test of every application."]}
